00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:03.350 align:start position:0%
 
european union leaders have announced  sanctions against belarus after it  forcibly diverted a ryanair flight and  arrested a dissident journalist the  measures were agreed on at a summit in  brussels  member states decided to take the  following steps well belarusian airlines  will be banned from using eu airspace  and  airports economic sanctions and  travel restrictions will be imposed on a  number of individuals linked to the  plains diversions  and a three billion euro eu investment  package for belarus will remain  on hold now the european council is  demanding the immediate release of  detained journalist roman pratseyevich  his supporters fear for his safety  this is the beginning of a highly  contested video  dissident journalist ramon partasievic  appears in front of a camera  after his detention by belarusian  authorities  the video was posted monday evening to a  pro-government social media channel  pataciewicz is saying that he is in good  health at the end of the video  he is quote confessing to organizing  mass disturbances  in minsk but patacievich's supporters  such as exiled opposition leader  svetlana tyranovskaya  believed the video was made under duress  he said  that he is being treated locally but he  is clearly beaten  and under pressure there is no doubt  that he may be tortured he was taken  hostage  the eu was firm in its response  introducing punitive measures against  the regime in minsk  top of the list banning belarusian  airlines from european skies  the eu is also slapping sanctions on  those responsible  for protesters abduction western powers  have widely condemned the incident  including nato the forced landing  of a passenger flight by belarus was  dangerous  and unacceptable this is a state  hijacking and demonstrates how the  regime means attacks  basic democratic rights on sunday  ramen protech was traveling from greece  to lithuania on orion airplane  the commercial flight he was on was  re-routed on its way to vilnius  and then escorted to minsk by a  belarusian warplane  allegedly because of a bomb threat but  no explosives were found on the aircraft  as a co-founder and former editor of the  nether channel on telegram  prataceevic used his influential voice  to royal alexander lukashenko's regime  he now faces up to 15 years in prison  let's take a closer look at this with  franco fertilka he's an advisor to  belarus his  opposition leader svetlana chikanovskaya  welcome to dw  um what do you make of this video  so i think he spent 24 hours in  lukashenko's custody in the hands of kgb  and he this confessed in the video  this this is the result of the uh  pressure of the psychological potential  the physical pressure  uh we know that kgb i saw this video  already in august and september when  thousands of billions got detained after  mass marches  and i must assure you that the  belarusian kgb can can force you to tell  whatever they want  right so as far as you're concerned this  is worthless because  this is a forced confession  exactly exactly and the good thing about  this that he is alive  we heard we heard and we saw that he is  alive  he is not very healthy and definitely  his uh  life is still under threat i think  rahman prathasevic is doing the  right thing when he is speaking what  they wanted him to say  otherwise he can be physically  intimidated always when we speak about  hostages safety  hostage must follow what the the  the criminal is doing our goal is to  release him as soon as possible  and to make sure that those who  who captured the hostage will pay high  price  how much do you think the eu's sanctions  will help to get him free  what we saw it was the wake-up call call  for international community for the  european union the united states  lukashenko was using the lack of  reaction from the west for many years  it created the feeling of impunity he  was arresting opponents he was cracking  down on  media he was detaining journalists and  right now after the plane situation  happened on sunday  we saw very quick reaction of brussels  of berlin and washington of london  this is a very good sign but only uh  closing the airspace is not enough it's  very important to isolate lukashenka's  regime  politically and diplomatically it's very  important that all perpetrators judges  prosecutors  all dues organize the special operation  they will be punished  and it's very important to discuss  economic sanctions but also  international investigation and tribunal  right so those would be your priorities  um and you would call on the eu to do  that  now or do or do would you like to see  them wait and see if this has  actually has any effect  we call on the eu to act now to act  quickly to act  bravely it's enough is enough lukashenka  shows that he  neglects all the rules all the  regulations  uh it's very good that eu reacted right  now but in order to stop the regime  the strong action joint effort needed  between all major stakeholders because  now it's not the domestic political  issue of belarus only  this is the threat to entire europe and  this is why  action isolation of the regime is  important  today thank you so much for for making  time for us belarusian opposition  advisor uh frank  facchoka  thank you well after last night's  meeting in brussels german chancellor  angela merkel dismissed  belarus's explanation of the incident  and had this message for the leadership  in minsk  must be released immediately all the  explanations for the first landing of  the ryanair flight are implausible  the same applies to his partner sophia  sapega we are demanding their release  zapiega more german reaction than from  andreas nick who is a member of the  german parliament for chancellor  merkel's christian democrats he also  sits on the parliament's  foreign affairs committee welcome to dw  um  will these sanctions from the eu cause  president lukashenko  to release mr president  well we certainly hope so we demand his  immediate release and  also that of sophia sabiga what we have  seen is i think an unprecedented violent  interference to  civil aviation in europe by a state  actor  some have even described it as an act of  state terrorism i think this is totally  unacceptable  we must be very strict and very clear in  our response to make sure that we don't  see contagion  of that kind of behavior to other  authoritarian regimes  around the world this is totally  unacceptable it was the interception of  a flight between  the capitals of two eu and two nato  member states  and this uh deserves the the clearest  and strictest response  uh possible but here's the thing about  that that response  you've all the eu already has sanctions  against uh  against uh belarus nothing has happened  there is  nothing about the sanctions there is  nothing about the actions of  of president lukashenko that it gives  any indication that  sanctions will have any effect so where  does this hope  that this will help to bring about the  the relief of  release of mr president had come from  i think the the required release of mr  porter  and his uh his girlfriend is is one  thing but we also need to be absolutely  clear in uh counteracting against that  violent interference into international  air travel we have now de facto turned  belarus into a civilian no-fly zone  within 24 hours  airspace is closed civilian airliners  from  western europe will not uh go to minsk  anymore  we've asked for an ikea investigation  and and i think this is a very strict  first response i think  in the second step we will have to  target state enterprises  to a more severe extent whether it's in  energy or chemicals or other industrial  sectors  i think there is still scope for uh very  clear messages and clear measures  towards belarus  belarus has now really made itself the  ultimate rogue state  in europe it is the only state not  eligible for membership in the council  of europe because it maintains  the death penalty which may hopefully  not be an issue in this  specific context as far as mr proceed  which is concerned  but this is totally unacceptable this is  an interference into  sovereignty of eu member states and nato  member states and we need to be as clear  as possible in our response  i'd like to get your reaction to  something that we we heard uh yesterday  from uh britain's uh a foreign uh  minister  uh dominican rabb he said it's very  difficult to believe that this kind of  action  would have been taken without without at  least the acquiescence of the  authorities  in moscow i wonder do you also  believe russia to be involved in this  i have no specific evidence for that but  i think there is a lot of plausibility  that  also operationally this may not be  something that belarus has done  on their own and we hear  concerning messages from moscow in this  whole regard we understand that mr  lukashenko  is in the hands of of of the kremlin and  has their  their backing but we also need to make  clear that this comes at a cost  uh to everyone involved as well good  talking to you thank you so much for  joining us andreas nick from uh  the german parliament's foreign affairs  committee  thank you

